WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.910
Public-key cryptography uses key pairs with different roles.

2
00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:06.049
A public key can be shared broadly.

3
00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:08.778
A private key must be protected by its owner.

4
00:00:08.778 --> 00:00:16.892
Depending on the scheme and protocol, these keys may support signatures, verification, key agreement, or encryption workflows.

5
00:00:16.892 --> 00:00:22.720
The application should not assume that any public-key operation can substitute for another.

6
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.588
Digital signatures provide verifiable authenticity and integrity for data.

7
00:00:27.588 --> 00:00:36.514
Verification shows that the data was signed by the holder of the corresponding private key, assuming the public key is trusted for that purpose.

8
00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:41.677
It does not automatically prove authorization, freshness, or business approval.

9
00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:45.587
Those meanings come from the surrounding protocol and policy.

10
00:00:45.587 --> 00:00:48.463
Certificates bind identities to public keys.

11
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:54.881
A certificate chain connects a peer or signer to trust anchors accepted by the application or platform.

12
00:00:54.881 --> 00:01:04.692
The application should know which trust anchors it uses, how they are updated, what identities are expected, and which certificate purposes are acceptable.

13
00:01:04.692 --> 00:01:06.978
Trust decisions should be explicit.

14
00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:12.289
A key trusted for test signing should not become a production signing key by accident.

15
00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:19.002
A certificate trusted for one service identity should not automatically authorize unrelated operations.

16
00:01:19.002 --> 00:01:23.575
Verification should match the intended purpose, context, and identity.

17
00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:26.969
Public-key operations also have lifecycle concerns.

18
00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:36.410
Private keys need protection, public keys need distribution, certificates expire, trust anchors change, and signature formats may need versioning.

19
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:46.885
Reviewers should look for clear ownership, safe verification failure behavior, and tests that exercise invalid, expired, wrong-purpose, and wrong-identity cases.
